# GOV-004
# Digital Engineering Signatures Specification

**Specification ID:** GOV-004

**Title:** Digital Engineering Signatures Specification

**Status:** Draft 1.0

**Authority:** Open Engineering Platform Specification

**Depends On:**

- GOV-001 Engineering Governance Architecture
- GOV-002 Engineering Review Boards & Governing Authorities
- GOV-003 Engineering Governance Workflow
- PKG-005 Package Trust & Digital Signature Specification

---

# 1. Purpose

This specification defines how engineering approvals, reviews, certifications, and governance decisions are digitally signed within the Open Engineering Platform.

Engineering Signatures provide verifiable evidence that an authorized individual or governing authority approved a specific engineering action.

They establish accountability, authenticity, integrity, and non-repudiation.

---

# 2. Design Goals

Engineering Signatures shall be:

- Cryptographically verifiable
- Attributable
- Immutable
- Auditable
- Policy-aware
- Platform independent
- Long-term verifiable

---

# 3. Engineering Signature Philosophy

A Digital Engineering Signature is more than proof of identity.

It represents an engineering decision.

Every signature asserts:

"I intentionally approved this engineering artifact under the authority granted to me."

Signatures therefore become governance artifacts.

---

# 4. Scope

Engineering Signatures may be applied to:

Engineering Objects

Requirements

Specifications

Packages

Repository Changes

Workflow Approvals

Engineering Decisions

Policies

Review Records

Meeting Minutes

Validation Reports

Organizations may extend signature coverage.

---

# 5. Signature Components

Every Engineering Signature shall include:

Signature ID

Signer Identity

Authority

Organization

Timestamp

Artifact Identifier

Artifact Version

Workflow Identifier

Decision

Cryptographic Signature

Signature Algorithm

Certificate Reference

Policy Reference

Supporting Evidence References

---

# 6. Signature Identity

Signatures are associated with verified identities.

Identity may represent:

Individual Engineer

Reviewer

Chairperson

Publisher

Organization Representative

Automated Service Account

Governing Authority (where organizational policy permits)

Identity verification is implementation-defined.

---

# 7. Signature Intent

Every signature shall record its purpose.

Examples include:

Reviewed

Approved

Rejected

Validated

Verified

Certified

Acknowledged

Released

Superseded

Revoked

Intent is immutable.

---

# 8. Multi-Signature Support

Artifacts may require multiple signatures.

Examples:

Engineer

↓

Technical Reviewer

↓

Quality Assurance

↓

Safety Review

↓

Engineering Review Board Chair

↓

Release Authority

Organizations define required signature sequences.

---

# 9. Signature Policies

Organizations may define signature policies including:

Required Roles

Required Authorities

Minimum Signature Count

Approval Order

Expiration Rules

Delegation Rules

Emergency Procedures

Policy evaluation occurs before signatures are accepted.

---

# 10. Cryptographic Requirements

Engineering Signatures shall use approved cryptographic algorithms.

Algorithms shall support:

Integrity

Authenticity

Non-repudiation

Algorithm selection is defined by platform security standards.

Future algorithms may be adopted without changing this specification.

---

# 11. Signature Validation

Validation confirms:

Signer Identity

Certificate Validity

Authority Assignment

Artifact Integrity

Workflow Compliance

Policy Compliance

Timestamp Integrity

Invalid signatures shall never be silently accepted.

---

# 12. Signature Revocation

A signature itself is never deleted.

If organizational policy requires withdrawal, the signature record remains while a revocation record is added.

Revocation records shall include:

Reason

Authority

Timestamp

Related Signature

Supporting Evidence

---

# 13. Delegated Signatures

Organizations may permit delegated signing authority.

Delegated signatures shall record:

Delegating Authority

Delegate

Scope

Effective Period

Restrictions

Delegation does not transfer ultimate accountability.

---

# 14. Long-Term Verification

Engineering Signatures shall remain verifiable even after:

Certificate Renewal

Software Updates

Repository Migration

Exchange Synchronization

Long-term verification mechanisms are implementation-defined.

---

# 15. Signature Evidence

Supporting evidence may include:

Review Comments

Meeting Minutes

Validation Reports

Test Results

Simulation Outputs

Compliance Documents

Engineering Calculations

Evidence becomes part of the signature record.

---

# 16. Events

Examples:

Signature Requested

Signature Applied

Signature Validated

Signature Rejected

Signature Revoked

Certificate Updated

Delegation Granted

Delegation Expired

Events become permanent governance records.

---

# 17. Audit

Signature audit records preserve:

Who Signed

What Was Signed

Why It Was Signed

Authority Exercised

Workflow Stage

Policy Applied

Timestamp

Verification Status

Audit history is immutable.

---

# 18. Future Extensions

Future specifications may introduce:

Hardware security modules

Biometric confirmation

Remote witnessing

Threshold cryptography

Quantum-resistant algorithms

Cross-organization signatures

Distributed approval protocols

without altering the Engineering Signature model.

---

# 19. Conformance

An implementation claiming compliance with GOV-004 shall:

- Support cryptographically verifiable engineering signatures.
- Preserve immutable signature history.
- Support multi-signature workflows.
- Validate signatures against governance policies.
- Preserve long-term verifiability.
- Support signature revocation records.
- Maintain complete signature audit trails.